\title{The Oracle Overstates the Deployable Win:\\
An Information Barrier for Streaming Attention-Cache Eviction}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{2 September 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
Trained transformer attention is extremely concentrated: for a query row of a
trained model, a cache holding only the $32$ keys of largest attention weight
retains $99.13\%$ of that row's probability mass, and $64$ keys retain
$99.53\%$, in a context of $1024$. These figures are obtained by an
\emph{oracle} selector that chooses the cache after reading the row it must
serve. We show that they do not transfer to any deployable selector, and we
quantify the failure exactly.

We model a cache as a set $S$ of at most $B$ key indices out of $n$, and its
retention on query row $t$ as $\kept(t,S)=\sum_{j\in S}w_t(j)$, where $w_t$ is
the attention distribution of row $t$. The oracle value is the maximum of
$\kept(t,\cdot)$ over admissible caches. A \emph{causally honest policy} is a
map from attention matrices and row indices to admissible caches depending only
on rows strictly earlier than $t$.

On the positive side, an exchange lemma shows that the cache formed by the top
$B$ keys under an arbitrary score $s$ is oracle-optimal whenever $s$ is
order-consistent with the served row, and loses at most $B\varepsilon$ under
$\varepsilon$-approximate consistency; an explicit instance attains
$B\varepsilon$, so the price is sharp. On the negative side we prove that for
every $n$, every budget $1\le B<n$ and \emph{every} causally honest policy $P$
there is an attention matrix, all of whose rows are genuine probability
distributions, on which the oracle retains $1$ and $P$ retains $0$: the
oracle-to-policy gap is $1$, the maximum possible. A counting identity gives an
average bound of $B/n$ over an explicit family of $n$ instances, and a
Yao-style averaging extends it to arbitrary finite mixtures, so randomisation
does not help. Block-granular eviction does not change the separation; per-layer
budget allocation is a min-plus convolution through which a per-layer penalty
$\delta$ survives, accumulating to $L\delta$ over $L$ layers; and the gap
vanishes only in the degenerate regime $B=n$. Finally, two diagnostic families
show that neither pure accumulation nor pure recency dominates, and that a
budget split retains both families precisely when both halves are nonzero, which
makes the empirically superior hybrid policy structurally forced rather than
tuned.
\end{abstract}

\noindent\textbf{Keywords:} attention cache eviction, heavy hitters, oracle gap,
online algorithms, exchange argument, min-plus convolution, information barrier.

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{The suitcase problem}

An autoregressive transformer serving a context of $n$ tokens maintains a
key--value record per token per attention head. Generating each new token
requires attending over all of them, so memory and bandwidth scale linearly in
the context. \emph{Cache eviction} --- discarding all but $B\ll n$ records --- is
the standard remedy, and its central empirical justification is the observed
concentration of trained attention.

The justification is usually presented as a retention curve: for each query row
$t$, the fraction of row mass captured by the $B$ keys of largest weight. Such
curves show a sharp knee, already above $0.99$ at budgets on the order of
$2$--$6\%$ of the context. It is tempting to read these curves as forecasts of
what an eviction policy will deliver at that budget. They are not, and the
discrepancy is large. On the measurement motivating this work --- a
$0.5$-billion-parameter model at context $1024$, block-$128$ eviction, strict
per-row causality, matched budgets, identical data for all arms --- the observed
retentions were:

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{lccc}
\toprule
arm & $B=32$ & $B=64$ & $B=128$\\
\midrule
oracle (per-row top-$k$) & $0.9913$ & $0.9953$ & ---\\
accumulated heavy hitters & $0.8633$ & $0.8822$ & $0.9189$\\
hybrid (heavy hitters $+$ recency) & $0.9205$ & $0.9384$ & $0.9605$\\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

Three pre-registered hypotheses were tested. \textbf{P1}, that the
oracle-to-policy gap would exceed a $2\%$ floor at matched budget, was confirmed
emphatically: at $B=64$ the gap is $0.9953-0.8822=0.1131$. \textbf{P2}, that a
recency reserve improves on pure accumulation, was confirmed at every budget
($+5.72$, $+5.62$, $+4.16$ points). \textbf{P3}, that some deployable arm would
reach $0.95$ at $B=64$, was refuted: the best deployable value there is
$0.9384$, and even a cache of $12.5\%$ of the context reaches only $0.9605$. The
oracle arm replicates an independent earlier measurement of the same knee to
four decimals ($0.9913$ against $0.9912$ at $k=32$), which anchors the harness.

\subsection{Contribution}

This paper turns those three empirical horns into theorems about set-valued
cache policies. The measured $11.3$-point gap is the mild, average-case shadow
of a worst-case separation of a full unit. Concretely: an exchange lemma
characterising when score-ranked caches are optimal, with an exact
$B\varepsilon$ price for approximate consistency and a matching instance
(\S\ref{sec:exchange}); an impossibility theorem for all causally honest
policies, deterministic or randomised (\S\ref{sec:impossible}); a structural
account of why hybrids win (\S\ref{sec:hybrid}); closure of three engineering
escapes (\S\ref{sec:escapes}); and a deployment recipe (\S\ref{sec:deploy}).

What is new is not the observation that policies underperform oracles, but the
quantification: the gap is exhibited as an information barrier holding for every
causal policy at once; its worst-case size is determined exactly; the assumption
that removes it is isolated exactly; and the price of approximate satisfaction
of that assumption is computed exactly and shown attained.

\section{Setting and definitions}

Throughout, $n$ is the number of key positions, indexed $0,\dots,n-1$, and $B$
is a cache budget.

\begin{definition}[Attention matrix]
An \emph{attention matrix} is a function $w:\mathbb{N}\times\mathbb{N}\to\mathbb{R}$,
written $w_t(j)$, the probability that query row $t$ places on key $j$. The
instances below are \emph{stochastic}: $w_t(j)\ge 0$ and $\sum_{j<n}w_t(j)=1$.
General theorems require only nonnegativity where stated.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Retention]
For a finite $S\subseteq\mathbb{N}$, $\displaystyle \kept(w,t,S)=\sum_{j\in S}w_t(j)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Admissible caches]
$\Caches(n,B)=\{S\subseteq\{0,\dots,n-1\}: |S|\le B\}$, a finite nonempty family
(it contains $\emptyset$).
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Oracle]
$\displaystyle \oracle(n,B,w,t)=\max_{S\in\Caches(n,B)}\kept(w,t,S)$.
Two facts are used constantly: $\kept(w,t,S)\le\oracle(n,B,w,t)$ for admissible
$S$, and if $\kept(w,t,S)\le c$ for all admissible $S$ then
$\oracle(n,B,w,t)\le c$. The maximum is taken \emph{after} row $t$ is revealed;
this is the entire difference between the oracle and a policy.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Causally honest policy]\label{def:causal}
A map $P$ from attention matrices and row indices to finite sets is
\emph{causally honest at $(n,B)$} if
\begin{enumerate}[label=(\roman*)]
\item (admissibility) $P(w,t)\in\Caches(n,B)$ for all $w,t$; and
\item (causality) $w_r(j)=w'_r(j)$ for all $r<t$ and all $j$ implies
$P(w,t)=P(w',t)$.
\end{enumerate}
Clause (ii) is the formal content of ``deployable''. The oracle is not of this
form.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Accumulated score; the three policies]
$\acc_t(j)=\sum_{r<t}w_r(j)$. Write $\topB_B(s)$ for the set of the $B$
highest-scoring keys under $s$ (ties broken by index; made precise in
\S\ref{sec:scoreranked}). Then the \emph{heavy-hitter cache} is
$H_B(w,t)=\topB_B(\acc_t)$; the \emph{recency cache} is
$R_m=\{n-m,\dots,n-1\}$; the \emph{hybrid cache} is
$H_{\lfloor B/2\rfloor}(w,t)\cup R_{B-\lfloor B/2\rfloor}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{proposition}
All three policies are causally honest at their budgets.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Admissibility: $|\topB_B(s)|\le B$ by construction, $|R_m|\le m$ as $R_m$ is an
interval of length $\min(m,n)$, and $|X\cup Y|\le|X|+|Y|$ handles the hybrid.
Causality: $\acc_t$ depends only on rows $r<t$, so equal prefixes give equal
scores and hence equal top-$B$ sets; $R_m$ reads no row at all.
\end{proof}

\section{Retrospective pruning is easy: the exchange lemma}\label{sec:exchange}

\begin{theorem}[Exchange lemma, approximate form]\label{thm:exchange}
Let $w_t\ge 0$ pointwise, $\varepsilon\ge 0$, and let $H,S$ be finite key sets
with $|S|\le|H|$. Suppose
\[
\forall k\in S\setminus H,\ \forall j\in H\setminus S:\qquad
w_t(k)\le w_t(j)+\varepsilon .
\]
Then $\kept(w,t,S)\le \kept(w,t,H)+|H|\varepsilon$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Split both sums along the common part: the contributions of $S\cap H$ and
$H\cap S$ coincide, so it suffices to bound $\sum_{S\setminus H}w_t$ by
$\sum_{H\setminus S}w_t+|H|\varepsilon$. If $H\setminus S=\emptyset$, the
cardinality identity $|S\setminus H|+|S\cap H|=|S|\le|H|=|H\setminus S|+|H\cap S|$
forces $S\setminus H=\emptyset$ and the claim is trivial. Otherwise choose $j_0$
minimising $w_t$ on $H\setminus S$. Every $k\in S\setminus H$ obeys
$w_t(k)\le w_t(j_0)+\varepsilon$, so
$\sum_{S\setminus H}w_t\le|S\setminus H|\,(w_t(j_0)+\varepsilon)$. The same
cardinality identity gives $|S\setminus H|\le|H\setminus S|$, minimality gives
$|H\setminus S|\,w_t(j_0)\le\sum_{H\setminus S}w_t$, and nonnegativity absorbs
the remainder; the slack is $|H\setminus S|\varepsilon\le|H|\varepsilon$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Exact form]
With $\varepsilon=0$: if $|S|\le|H|$ and every key of $S\setminus H$ is worth no
more than every key of $H\setminus S$, then $\kept(w,t,S)\le\kept(w,t,H)$. Hence
the top-$B$ set by the \emph{true} row is an optimal cache: retrospective
pruning is a one-line greedy.
\end{corollary}

\subsection{Score-ranked caches}\label{sec:scoreranked}

To make ``top $B$'' well defined under ties, order keys by the strict total order
\[
m\succ_s j\quad:\Longleftrightarrow\quad s(j)<s(m)\ \text{ or }\ \bigl(s(m)=s(j)\ \text{and}\ m<j\bigr),
\]
which is irreflexive, transitive and total on distinct indices. Put
$\rank_n(s,j)=|\{m<n: m\succ_s j\}|$ and
$\topB_B(s)=\{j<n:\rank_n(s,j)<B\}$. Since $\rank_n(s,\cdot)$ is injective on
$\{0,\dots,n-1\}$ and bounded by $n$, we get $|\topB_B(s)|\le B$, with equality
when $B\le n$, so $\topB_B(s)\in\Caches(n,B)$. Moreover if $j\in\topB_B(s)$ and
$k<n$ with $k\notin\topB_B(s)$ then $s(k)\le s(j)$: otherwise $j$ would rank
strictly below $k$, contradicting $\rank(j)<B\le\rank(k)$.

\subsection{Consistency and the exact price of its failure}

\begin{definition}[Order consistency]
A score $s$ is \emph{consistent} with a value vector $v$ on $\{0,\dots,n-1\}$ if
$s(k)\le s(j)\Rightarrow v(k)\le v(j)$ for all $j,k<n$; it is
\emph{$\varepsilon$-consistent} if $s(k)\le s(j)\Rightarrow v(k)\le v(j)+\varepsilon$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Approximate consistency is priced at $B\varepsilon$]\label{thm:price}
Let $w_t\ge 0$, $\varepsilon\ge 0$, $B\le n$, and let $s$ be
$\varepsilon$-consistent with $w_t$ on $\{0,\dots,n-1\}$. Then
\[
\oracle(n,B,w,t)\ \le\ \kept\bigl(w,t,\topB_B(s)\bigr)+B\varepsilon .
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Put $H=\topB_B(s)$, so $|H|=B$. For admissible $S$ we have $|S|\le B=|H|$, and
for $k\in S\setminus H$, $j\in H\setminus S$ the domination property of
\S\ref{sec:scoreranked} gives $s(k)\le s(j)$, whence
$w_t(k)\le w_t(j)+\varepsilon$. Theorem~\ref{thm:exchange} applies; take the
maximum over $S$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Assumption-conditioned optimality]\label{thm:consistent}
If $w_t\ge0$, $B\le n$ and $s$ is consistent with $w_t$, then
$\oracle(n,B,w,t)=\kept(w,t,\topB_B(s))$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
``$\le$'' is Theorem~\ref{thm:price} with $\varepsilon=0$; ``$\ge$'' is
admissibility of $\topB_B(s)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[The price is sharp]\label{thm:sharp}
Let $\varepsilon\ge 0$ and $2B\le n$. Take $s(j)=-j$, so
$\topB_B(s)=\{0,\dots,B-1\}$, and the instance $w_t(j)=0$ for $j<B$ and
$w_t(j)=\varepsilon$ for $j\ge B$. Then $\kept(w,t,\topB_B(s))=0$, the instance
is $\varepsilon$-consistent with $s$, and
$\oracle(n,B,w,t)=B\varepsilon=\kept(w,t,\topB_B(s))+B\varepsilon$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Upper bound: every key is worth at most $\varepsilon$ and a cache holds at most
$B$ keys. Lower bound: the block $\{B,\dots,2B-1\}$ is admissible (here
$2B\le n$ is needed) and each of its $B$ keys carries $\varepsilon$. Consistency:
any two weights differ by at most $\varepsilon$.
\end{proof}

Thus no conversion of score quality into retention beats $B\varepsilon$, and the
correction scales with the \emph{cache}, not the \emph{context}.

\subsection{The hypothesis behind heavy hitters, isolated}

\begin{theorem}[Exact stationarity]\label{thm:stat}
Suppose $w_t\ge0$, $B\le n$, and there are constants $c\ge0$, $d$ with
$w_t(j)=c\,\acc_t(j)+d$ for all $j<n$. Then
$\oracle(n,B,w,t)=\kept(w,t,H_B(w,t))$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
An order-preserving affine map turns $\acc_t(k)\le\acc_t(j)$ into
$w_t(k)\le w_t(j)$, so $\acc_t$ is consistent with $w_t$; apply
Theorem~\ref{thm:consistent}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Approximate stationarity]
If $|w_t(j)-(c\,\acc_t(j)+d)|\le\varepsilon/2$ for all $j<n$ with $c\ge0$,
$\varepsilon\ge0$, then $\oracle(n,B,w,t)\le\kept(w,t,H_B(w,t))+B\varepsilon$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$\acc_t(k)\le\acc_t(j)$ gives
$w_t(k)\le c\,\acc_t(k)+d+\varepsilon/2\le c\,\acc_t(j)+d+\varepsilon/2\le w_t(j)+\varepsilon$,
i.e.\ $\varepsilon$-consistency; apply Theorem~\ref{thm:price}.
\end{proof}

These two theorems name what heavy-hitter eviction silently assumes: that the
row about to be served is, up to an order-preserving affine distortion, the row
already seen on average.

\section{Online prediction is impossible}\label{sec:impossible}

\subsection{The adversarial family}

\begin{definition}
For $n\ge1$, $T\ge0$, $j_0<n$, let
\[
\adv(n,T,j_0)_t(j)=
\begin{cases}
1/n, & t<T,\ j<n,\\
1, & t\ge T,\ j=j_0,\\
0, & \text{otherwise.}
\end{cases}
\]
The first $T$ rows are uniform; the served row $T$ is a one-hot at $j_0$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Legitimacy]
For $0<n$ and $j_0<n$, every row of $\adv(n,T,j_0)$ is a probability
distribution.
\end{lemma}

\begin{lemma}[Indistinguishable prefixes]\label{lem:prefix}
For all $j_0,j_1<n$, all $r<T$ and all $j$:
$\adv(n,T,j_0)_r(j)=\adv(n,T,j_1)_r(j)$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{lemma}[Served row]\label{lem:served}
$\kept(\adv(n,T,j_0),T,S)=1$ if $j_0\in S$, and $0$ otherwise.
\end{lemma}

\begin{theorem}[The oracle retains everything]\label{thm:oracleadv}
For $B\ge1$ and $j_0<n$, $\oracle(n,B,\adv(n,T,j_0),T)=1$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Upper bound from Lemma~\ref{lem:served}; lower bound because $\{j_0\}$ is
admissible when $B\ge1$.
\end{proof}

\subsection{The impossibility}

\begin{theorem}[Every causal policy misses]\label{thm:misses}
Let $P$ be causally honest at $(n,B)$ with $B<n$. Then there is $j_0<n$ with
$\kept(\adv(n,T,j_0),T,P(\adv(n,T,j_0),T))=0$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Let $S=P(\adv(n,T,0),T)$, so $S\subseteq\{0,\dots,n-1\}$ and $|S|\le B<n$; hence
some $j_0<n$ lies outside $S$, for otherwise $n\le|S|\le B$. By
Lemma~\ref{lem:prefix} the instances $\adv(n,T,j_0)$ and $\adv(n,T,0)$ agree on
all rows $r<T$, so causality gives $P(\adv(n,T,j_0),T)=S$; Lemma~\ref{lem:served}
with $j_0\notin S$ gives retention $0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[The oracle overstates the deployable win]\label{thm:main}
Let $P$ be causally honest at $(n,B)$ with $1\le B<n$. Then there is $j_0<n$ with
\[
\oracle\bigl(n,B,\adv(n,T,j_0),T\bigr)-\kept\bigl(\adv(n,T,j_0),T,P(\adv(n,T,j_0),T)\bigr)=1 .
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Combine Theorems~\ref{thm:oracleadv} and~\ref{thm:misses}.
\end{proof}

Since retention lies in $[0,1]$, a gap of $1$ is maximal. Note the quantifier
order: the theorem is universally quantified over policies, so it covers schemes
not yet invented, including learned importance heads, provided only that they
read the past.

\begin{corollary}[No deployable heuristic is exempt]
At any $1\le B<n$, each of the heavy-hitter, recency and hybrid caches suffers a
full unit gap on some instance of the family.
\end{corollary}

\subsection{Averages and randomisation}

\begin{theorem}[Counting bound]\label{thm:count}
For any causally honest $P$ at $(n,B)$,
$\sum_{j_0<n}\kept(\adv(n,T,j_0),T,P(\adv(n,T,j_0),T))\le B$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
By causality $P$ outputs the same $S$ on every member, so the $j_0$-th summand is
the indicator $\mathbf 1[j_0\in S]$ and the sum is $|S|\le B$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
For $n>0$ the mean retention of a causal policy over the family is at most
$B/n$, while the oracle equals $1$ on every member.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[Randomisation does not help]\label{thm:yao}
Let $I$ be finite, $(P_i)_{i\in I}$ causally honest at $(n,B)$, and $q_i\ge0$
with $\sum_i q_i=1$. If $n>0$ there exists $j_0<n$ with
\[
\sum_{i\in I}q_i\,\kept\bigl(\adv(n,T,j_0),T,P_i(\adv(n,T,j_0),T)\bigr)\ \le\ \frac{B}{n}.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Sum over $j_0<n$ and exchange the order of summation; each inner sum is at most
$B$ by Theorem~\ref{thm:count}, so the double sum is at most
$\sum_i q_i B=B=n\cdot(B/n)$. A sum of $n$ terms bounded by $n$ copies of $B/n$
has a term at most $B/n$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Necessity of $B<n$]\label{thm:full}
For $w_t\ge0$, $\oracle(n,n,w,t)=\kept(w,t,\{0,\dots,n-1\})$: at full budget the
oracle's choice is vacuous and the gap is $0$.
\end{theorem}

\section{Why the hybrid, and why it is forced}\label{sec:hybrid}

\begin{definition}[Diagnostics]
With $n\ge1$, $T\ge1$: the \emph{stale} instance has $\stale_t=\delta_0$ for
$t<T$ and $\stale_T=\delta_{n-1}$; the \emph{pinned} instance has
$\pin_t=\delta_0$ for every $t$, including the served row.
\end{definition}

Their accumulated scores are immediate: on stale, $\acc_T(0)=T$ and $\acc_T(j)=0$
otherwise; on pinned, $\acc_t(0)=t$ and $\acc_t(j)=0$ otherwise.

\begin{theorem}[Accumulation is biased]\label{thm:hhstale}
For $1\le B\le n-1$ and $T\ge1$, $\kept(\stale,T,H_B(\stale,T))=0$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Key $0$ strictly outranks $n-1$ (score $T>0$ against $0$) and every other
$m\ne0$ ties with $n-1$ at score $0$, so ties break by index and $m\succ n-1$ for
all $m<n-1$. Hence $\rank(n-1)\ge B$, so $n-1\notin H_B$, while the served row's
entire mass sits on $n-1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Recency wins there]
For $m\ge1$, $\kept(\stale,T,R_m)=1$, since $n-1\in R_m$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Recency is biased too]\label{thm:recpin}
For $m\le n-1$, $\kept(\pin,t,R_m)=0$, since $R_m=\{n-m,\dots,n-1\}$ omits key
$0$, which carries the whole served row.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Accumulation wins there]\label{thm:hhpin}
For $B\ge1$, $\kept(\pin,t,H_B(\pin,t))=1$: no key outranks $0$, so $\rank(0)=0<B$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{corollary}[No dominance]
For $1\le B\le n-1$ and $T\ge1$, at the same budget on the same key set,
$\kept(\stale,T,H_B)<\kept(\stale,T,R_B)$ and
$\kept(\pin,T,R_B)<\kept(\pin,T,H_B)$, both comparisons being $0<1$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{definition}[Budget split]
$\spl_{a,b}(w,t)=H_a(w,t)\cup R_b$, of size at most $a+b$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[The hybrid split is forced]\label{thm:split}
Let $1\le a\le n-1$, $1\le b\le n-1$, $T\ge1$, $n\ge1$. Then
\begin{enumerate}[label=(\arabic*)]
\item $\kept(\stale,T,\spl_{a,b})=1$ and $\kept(\pin,T,\spl_{a,b})=1$;
\item $\kept(\stale,T,\spl_{a,0})=0$;
\item $\kept(\pin,T,\spl_{0,b})=0$.
\end{enumerate}
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
(1) The recency half contains $n-1$ when $b\ge1$; the heavy-hitter half contains
$0$ when $a\ge1$ by Theorem~\ref{thm:hhpin}. (2) With $b=0$, apply
Theorem~\ref{thm:hhstale}. (3) With $a=0$, apply Theorem~\ref{thm:recpin}.
\end{proof}

The hybrid is therefore the unique shape of policy passing both diagnostics,
which explains \textbf{P2} structurally. It also explains \textbf{P3}: the hybrid
is itself causally honest and so remains subject to Theorem~\ref{thm:main}.

\section{Three escapes, closed}\label{sec:escapes}

\subsection{Granularity}

Let $\blk(n,\beta,b)=\{j<n:\lfloor j/\beta\rfloor=b\}$ be the $b$-th block of
width $\beta\ge1$.

\begin{lemma}
$|\blk(n,\beta,b)|\le\beta$, since $j\mapsto j\bmod\beta$ is injective on a block
and lands in $\{0,\dots,\beta-1\}$. Hence $\blk(n,\beta,b)\in\Caches(n,B)$ when
$\beta\le B$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{theorem}[Block oracle still wins]\label{thm:blockoracle}
If $1\le\beta\le B$ and $j_0<n$, the block $\blk(n,\beta,\lfloor j_0/\beta\rfloor)$
is admissible and retains $1$ on $\adv(n,T,j_0)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[The wall is causality, not granularity]
For causally honest $P$ at $(n,B)$ with $B<n$ and any $1\le\beta\le B$, there is
$j_0<n$ with
\[
\kept\bigl(\adv(n,T,j_0),T,\blk(n,\beta,\lfloor j_0/\beta\rfloor)\bigr)
-\kept\bigl(\adv(n,T,j_0),T,P(\adv(n,T,j_0),T)\bigr)=1 .
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Theorem~\ref{thm:misses} supplies $j_0$ with policy retention $0$;
Theorem~\ref{thm:blockoracle} gives block-oracle retention $1$.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Per-layer allocation is a min-plus convolution}

\begin{definition}
For loss curves $f,g:\mathbb{N}\to\mathbb{R}$, the optimally allocated two-layer
loss at total budget $B$ is the min-plus (tropical) convolution
\[
(f\oplus g)(B)=\min_{0\le a\le B}\bigl(f(a)+g(B-a)\bigr),
\]
a minimum over a finite nonempty set, hence attained; it is commutative, since
$a\mapsto B-a$ is a bijection of the index range.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[$L$ layers]
$\alloc([\,])=0$, $\alloc([f])(B)=f(B)$, and
$\alloc(f::\mathrm{rest})(B)=\min_{0\le a\le B}\bigl(f(a)+\alloc(\mathrm{rest})(B-a)\bigr)$.
For two layers this is exactly $f\oplus g$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Gaps add across layers]\label{thm:layers}
If $f_\ell$ are oracle loss curves, $f'_\ell$ the corresponding deployable
curves, and $f_\ell(a)+\delta\le f'_\ell(a)$ for every layer $\ell$ and budget
$a$, then for every total budget $B$ and every list of $L$ layers
\[
\alloc(f_1,\dots,f_L)(B)+L\delta\ \le\ \alloc(f'_1,\dots,f'_L)(B).
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Induction on $L$. For $L=1$ it is the hypothesis. For the step let $a$ attain the
minimum for the primed list; bound the unprimed value by the same split, then
apply the hypothesis at $a$ for layer $1$ and the inductive hypothesis at budget
$B-a$ for the remaining $L-1$ layers, gaining $\delta$ and $(L-1)\delta$.
\end{proof}

No convexity or monotonicity of the loss curves is assumed, so the statement
covers irregular real per-layer profiles: reallocating a global budget across
depth cannot recover a per-layer policy penalty.

