\section{The recorded run as arithmetic}

Writing $\ret(\text{arm},B)$ for the tabulated retentions of \S1.1:
\textbf{P1} $\ret(\text{oracle},64)-\ret(\text{hh},64)=0.9953-0.8822=0.1131>0.02$;
\textbf{P2} $\ret(\text{hh},B)<\ret(\text{hyb},B)$ at $B=32,64,128$;
\textbf{P3 refuted} $\ret(\text{hyb},64)=0.9384<0.95$ and
$\ret(\text{hyb},128)=0.9605<0.97$. Every tabulated value lies in $[0,1]$ and each
arm is strictly increasing in budget.

Seven implementation variants were rejected by sanity gates before this run was
recorded: two shape errors; a stale kept-set starving local context (retention
$0.35$--$0.46$); a per-block causal leak letting rows see their own block's
future (retention $2.06>1$, impossible for a probability row); a masking
numerical failure; a duplicated recency reserve; and an unbound variable. The two
invalid variants serve as bracketing negative controls, one violating the
retention band from below and one from above.

\section{Deployment: what to correct and how}\label{sec:deploy}

\begin{enumerate}[label=\arabic*.]
\item \textbf{Quote oracle curves as upper bounds.} By Theorem~\ref{thm:main} the
oracle number is unattainable by any deployable evictor, and by
Theorem~\ref{thm:yao} not even by a randomised one.
\item \textbf{Measure the consistency defect.} For the score $s$ in use, define
\[
\varepsilon(w,s,t)=\max\{\,w_t(k)-w_t(j)\ :\ j,k<n,\ s(k)\le s(j)\,\}_+ ,
\]
the largest amount by which the score inverts the true row: a maximum over pairs
in a matrix the oracle arm already materialises.
\item \textbf{Apply the correction $B\varepsilon$ per layer.}
Theorem~\ref{thm:price} bounds the loss; Theorem~\ref{thm:sharp} shows the bound
attained, so it cannot be improved without further assumptions.
\item \textbf{Multiply by depth.} By Theorem~\ref{thm:layers} the per-layer
penalties add under optimal allocation.
\item \textbf{Keep both halves of the budget.} By Theorem~\ref{thm:split} an
all-heavy-hitter or all-recency split fails totally on an explicit family.
\end{enumerate}

If the defect of accumulated attention on trained models is $O(1/B)$, then
$B\varepsilon=O(1)$ with a small constant and the observed retention band is
\emph{forced} by Theorem~\ref{thm:price}. Conversely, a large measured defect
certifies that the oracle table cannot be repaired by any monotone reweighting of
the same score.

\section{Discussion}

\subsection{What the separation is, and is not}

It is an \emph{information} separation. Oracle and policy face the same
combinatorial problem --- choose $B$ of $n$ items maximising a linear objective ---
and that problem is trivial in both cases, solved in one line by the exchange
argument of \S\ref{sec:exchange}. The difference is the objective's
availability: on the adversarial family the prefix is statistically identical
across instances, and no amount of computation extracts information a prefix does
not contain.

It is not a claim that heavy-hitter eviction is a bad idea. On real text the
measured retention at $B=64$ is $0.8822$ for pure accumulation and $0.9384$ for
the hybrid, far from the worst-case $0$. Theorems~\ref{thm:stat} and following
say precisely what structure would make the estimator unbiased and how the loss
degrades as that structure fails.

\subsection{The measured gap versus the proved gap}

The proved worst case is $1$; the measurement is $0.1131$. The distance between
them is the remaining scientific content: which structural property of trained
attention makes the gap eleven points rather than a hundred, and can it be
certified online? Theorem~\ref{thm:price} reduces the question to a
one-dimensional estimation problem about $\varepsilon$.

\subsection{Limitations}

One model, one context length, one eviction granularity, no learned importance
head; corpus and scale robustness untested here. The theory scores a policy only
by single-row retained attention mass, a proxy for end-task quality. The
adversarial family is legitimate but not \emph{natural}: its rows are uniform and
then one-hot. This is a feature for an impossibility result and a limitation for
a forecast.

\section{Future work}

\begin{enumerate}[label=\arabic*.]
\item \textbf{The consistency defect as a deployment constant.} Measure
$\varepsilon(w,s,t)$ on trained models; if it is $O(1/B)$, oracle tables become
usable after a one-parameter correction.
\item \textbf{Online certificates.} Order consistency is a checkable property of
a prefix and a candidate ranking. Is there a cheap, necessarily conservative
online test certifying that pruning will transfer, enabling adaptive budgets?
\item \textbf{Learned importance heads.} A trained predictor is still a causal
policy and cannot beat Theorem~\ref{thm:main} in the worst case; the question is
whether it reduces the \emph{measured} defect.
\item \textbf{Per-layer budgets and depth.} Theorem~\ref{thm:layers} assumes only
pointwise penalties. Whether real per-layer loss curves are convex --- which is
what would make greedy allocation optimal --- is an open empirical question.
\end{enumerate}

Two further items are quantitative refinements: splitting the measured $11.3$
points into a coarsening term and a causality term, and replicating the table at
larger scale and on a broader corpus.

\section{Conclusion}

Concentration of trained attention is real and large: a $32$-key cache captures
$99.13\%$ of a row's attention mass and a $64$-key cache $99.53\%$, in a context
of $1024$. Both numbers come from a selector that has already read the row it
caches for. No selector restricted to the past can inherit them: for every budget
short of the full context and every causally honest policy, deterministic or
randomised, there is a legitimate stochastic instance on which the oracle retains
everything and the policy retains nothing, and on average over that family the
policy retains at most $B/n$. The escape is an assumption --- order-consistency of
the score with the future row --- whose exact price in the approximate regime is
$B\varepsilon$, attained. Neither block granularity nor per-layer reallocation
evades the barrier; the per-layer penalty accumulates linearly in depth. And the
empirically superior hybrid policy is structurally forced.

Trained attention is prunable in retrospect, not predictable in advance.
Deployment tables must be policy-adjusted; oracle quotes are upper bounds.

